<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippodrome of Constantinople</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hippodrome of Istanbul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hippodrome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>-racing stadium of the Byzantine capital, begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> and enlarged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>. It was the centre of the city's public life and of the Blue and Green factions, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a> of 532 began there. Its site is now Sultanahmet Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpent-column">Serpent Column</a> and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> still stand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hippodrome-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="Hippodrome of Constantinople" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninara · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultanahmet_Square,_Obelisk_of_Theodosius,_Istanbul_(52121868925).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The first Hippodrome was built when the city was still called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, a provincial town of moderate importance. In 203 CE the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> rebuilt the city and gave it a hippodrome. After his victory at the Battle of Chrysopolis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> refounded Byzantium in 324 as Nova Roma, a name that soon gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and he renovated the Hippodrome.</p><p>It is estimated to have been about 450 m long and 130 m wide, with starting gates, the carceres, at the northern end and the curved tribune, the sphendone, at the southern end of the U-shaped track. The stands held around 100,000 spectators. The emperor's lodge, the Kathisma, could be entered directly from the Great Palace by a passage reserved for the imperial family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Through the Byzantine period the Hippodrome was the centre of the city's social life, and large sums were wagered on the races. Four teams took part at first, each backed by a political party: the Blues, the Greens, the Reds and the Whites. The Reds and Whites weakened and were absorbed by the other two. The meetings were among the few occasions on which the emperor and ordinary citizens shared one place, and political matters were often discussed there.</p><p>The rivalry of the Blues and Greens often merged with political or religious disputes and sometimes produced riots amounting to civil war. The worst were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a> of 532, in which an estimated 30,000 people were killed and many buildings, including the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, destroyed. The last recorded race took place in 1200, and the sack of the city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> of 1204 ruined the Hippodrome. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who took the city in 1453, had no interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> racing, and the site served as a source of building stone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site is now Sultanahmet Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. The original track lies some 2 m below the present surface. Three monuments of the central barrier survive in excavated pits. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpent-column">Serpent Column</a> is the base of a tripod cast to celebrate the Greek victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in the 5th century BCE, which Constantine ordered moved from Delphi; its heads and top third were destroyed in 1700. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">Obelisk</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>, first raised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> about 1490 BCE, was brought by Theodosius the Great in 390, and its top section stands on a marble pedestal. The Walled Obelisk was built in the 10th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hippodrome of Constantinople” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…art and politics: the Athenian Treasury, the bronze Charioteer of Delphi of about 475 BCE, and the Serpent Column set up after the Battle of Plataea in 479 BCE, which Constantine the Great later moved to Constantinople, where its remains still stand in the old <b>Hippodrome of Istanbul</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>That church was burnt to the ground in January 532 in a fire started during the Nika Revolt, which had begun in the nearby <b>Hippodrome of Constantinople</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Justinian II, restored to the throne in 705 with the help of a Bulgarian army, had his predecessors Leontius and Tiberius III publicly executed in the <b>Hippodrome of Constantinople</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-15">15 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
